1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:03,623
It is the twenty second of January, two thousand and three.

2
00:00:03,623 --> 00:00:09,006
At the Johnson Space Center, an engineer named Rodney Rocha is typing an email he will not send.

3
00:00:10,133 --> 00:00:16,872
Six days earlier, eighty one point seven seconds after Columbia left the pad, a piece of insulating foam came off the fuel tank.

4
00:00:16,872 --> 00:00:20,974
Two tenths of a second later it struck the underside of the left wing.

5
00:00:22,099 --> 00:00:24,076
Nobody is sure how big it was.

6
00:00:24,076 --> 00:00:30,007
The cameras that should have recorded it were out of focus, or pointed the wrong way, or lost the vehicle altogether.

7
00:00:30,007 --> 00:00:32,548
So Rocha asks for a photograph of the wing.

8
00:00:33,666 --> 00:00:38,761
He asks on flight day four and gets no answer. He asks again on flight day six.

9
00:00:38,761 --> 00:00:45,055
And on the morning of the twenty second, he is told the Shuttle Program is not requesting any outside imaging help.

10
00:00:46,199 --> 00:00:51,036
So he writes it down. That this is the wrong answer. That it borders on irresponsible.

11
00:00:51,036 --> 00:00:52,547
And at the end, this.

12
00:00:53,666 --> 00:00:58,989
Remember the NASA safety posters everywhere around stating, if it's not safe, say so?

13
00:00:58,989 --> 00:01:00,510
Yes, it's that serious.

14
00:01:01,633 --> 00:01:05,551
He printed it out, carried it down the hall, and showed it to a colleague.

15
00:01:05,551 --> 00:01:06,596
He never sent it.

16
00:01:06,596 --> 00:01:11,037
Asked why, he told the accident board he did not want to jump the chain of command.

17
00:01:12,166 --> 00:01:22,615
Six months later, in San Antonio, engineers loaded a block of that foam into a nitrogen cannon built for testing bird strikes, and fired it at a shuttle wing at five hundred and thirty miles an hour.

18
00:01:23,733 --> 00:01:25,489
This is what it did.

19
00:01:25,489 --> 00:01:29,352
A hole roughly sixteen inches across, straight through the leading edge.

20
00:01:29,352 --> 00:01:33,215
The photograph Rodney Rocha asked for, three times, was never taken.

21
00:01:34,333 --> 00:01:37,415
Columbia. The photographs NASA never took.

22
00:01:38,533 --> 00:01:45,299
To understand why nobody took that photograph, you have to go back twenty two years, to the first time a space shuttle ever flew.

23
00:01:46,433 --> 00:01:47,852
April, nineteen eighty one.

24
00:01:47,852 --> 00:01:52,820
Columbia is the first orbiter, and this is the first flight of the programme.

25
00:01:52,820 --> 00:01:54,949
It comes back with damaged tiles.

26
00:01:56,066 --> 00:02:04,190
The external tank is covered in insulating foam, because it holds liquid hydrogen at two hundred and fifty three degrees below zero and it would otherwise ice over.

27
00:02:05,333 --> 00:02:10,219
The design requirement was explicit. The tank was not supposed to shed anything.

28
00:02:10,219 --> 00:02:12,099
Not foam, not ice, nothing.

29
00:02:13,233 --> 00:02:16,703
It shed foam on the first flight. And on the second.

30
00:02:16,703 --> 00:02:21,435
And it kept doing it, for twenty two years, on flight after flight after flight.

31
00:02:22,566 --> 00:02:29,645
There is a particular place on the tank called the left bipod ramp, a wedge of hand sprayed foam over the strut that holds the orbiter's nose.

32
00:02:30,766 --> 00:02:35,442
Before Columbia's last flight, foam had come off that exact ramp at least six times.

33
00:02:36,566 --> 00:02:38,768
But here is the part that matters.

34
00:02:38,768 --> 00:02:43,802
At the flight readiness review before Columbia launched, managers were told there had been only three.

35
00:02:44,933 --> 00:02:47,391
Two of the six had not been found yet.

36
00:02:47,391 --> 00:02:50,396
The accident board discovered them afterwards, going back through the record.

37
00:02:50,396 --> 00:02:54,494
The people approving the flight were working from a number that was wrong by half.

38
00:02:55,633 --> 00:02:57,059
December, nineteen eighty eight.

39
00:02:57,059 --> 00:03:03,835
Atlantis comes back from a classified mission with more than seven hundred damaged tiles and one tile missing altogether.

40
00:03:04,966 --> 00:03:08,920
The commander later said he believed they were going to die on re entry.

41
00:03:08,920 --> 00:03:15,415
The damage was directly beneath an antenna mounting plate, which was made of steel, and that is the only reason the wing survived.

42
00:03:16,533 --> 00:03:19,626
October, two thousand and two. Three months before Columbia.

43
00:03:19,626 --> 00:03:27,531
Foam comes off the left bipod ramp and hits the solid rocket booster attach ring, about four inches wide and three inches deep.

44
00:03:28,666 --> 00:03:33,480
That was the closest warning anyone got, and it arrived two missions before the accident.

45
00:03:33,480 --> 00:03:37,652
The programme classified it, flew twice more, and did not fix the ramp.

46
00:03:38,799 --> 00:03:43,258
There is a word for what was happening, and the accident board used it.

47
00:03:43,258 --> 00:03:47,080
NASA had two categories for problems. In family, and out of family.

48
00:03:48,199 --> 00:03:52,789
In family meant a problem you had seen before, within the experience base.

49
00:03:52,789 --> 00:03:54,907
Out of family meant something new.

50
00:03:54,907 --> 00:04:00,556
Foam loss had been seen so many times that it had become, by definition, in family.

51
00:04:01,699 --> 00:04:06,622
The board called that a strange term indeed for a violation of system requirements.

52
00:04:06,622 --> 00:04:10,138
Because the classification had been introduced after Challenger, to help.

53
00:04:10,138 --> 00:04:14,709
And by definition, the problems moved into the lesser category got less attention.

54
00:04:15,833 --> 00:04:19,085
The sociologist who worked on this report has a name for it.

55
00:04:19,085 --> 00:04:20,169
The normalisation of deviance.

56
00:04:20,169 --> 00:04:26,674
You see the thing that should not happen, and it does not kill you, so it stops being the thing that should not happen.

57
00:04:27,799 --> 00:04:34,800
On the sixteenth of January, two thousand and three, seven people boarded that orbiter for a sixteen day science mission.

58
00:04:35,933 --> 00:04:40,836
Rick Husband, commander. William McCool, pilot. Michael Anderson, payload commander.

59
00:04:40,836 --> 00:04:44,758
Kalpana Chawla, flight engineer. David Brown. Laurel Clark.

60
00:04:44,758 --> 00:04:48,681
And Ilan Ramon, the first Israeli in space.

61
00:04:49,799 --> 00:04:53,718
It was not a station flight. There was no docking, no rendezvous.

62
00:04:53,718 --> 00:05:00,248
Columbia carried a laboratory module in the payload bay and the crew ran experiments around the clock in two shifts.

63
00:05:01,366 --> 00:05:03,423
That detail matters more than it sounds.

64
00:05:03,423 --> 00:05:10,770
Because Columbia was not going to the space station, it had no robotic arm fitted, and there was nowhere to shelter if anything went wrong.

65
00:05:11,899 --> 00:05:15,373
Eighty one point seven seconds after launch, the foam came off.

66
00:05:16,500 --> 00:05:20,893
Nobody saw it happen. Not in the control room, not on the pad, not anywhere.

67
00:05:20,893 --> 00:05:24,701
It was found the next day, when the routine film review caught it.

68
00:05:25,833 --> 00:05:29,386
And when they went looking for a clear picture of it, they did not have one.

69
00:05:30,533 --> 00:05:35,391
Of the ground camera sites that should have recorded the ascent, the first lost track of the vehicle.

70
00:05:35,391 --> 00:05:38,631
The second was out of focus because of an improperly maintained lens.

71
00:05:38,631 --> 00:05:41,060
The third caught only the top of the wing.

72
00:05:42,199 --> 00:05:45,726
The accident board added one sentence to that finding.

73
00:05:45,726 --> 00:05:48,468
Camera problems also hindered the Challenger investigation.

74
00:05:49,599 --> 00:05:54,928
So on the seventeenth of January, the chair of the photo working group picked up the phone.

75
00:05:54,928 --> 00:05:59,003
The strike was classified out of family. Something new. Something nobody had seen.

76
00:06:00,133 --> 00:06:02,259
A team was stood up to analyse it.

77
00:06:02,259 --> 00:06:06,246
It was called the debris assessment team, and it was co chaired by Rodney Rocha.

78
00:06:07,366 --> 00:06:12,353
Over the next five days, three separate people asked for a photograph of that wing.

79
00:06:12,353 --> 00:06:14,680
Three separate requests, through three separate channels.

80
00:06:15,800 --> 00:06:21,206
The board counted them, and printed them in a box in the report, so there could be no argument about it afterwards.

81
00:06:22,333 --> 00:06:24,292
Request one. Flight day two.

82
00:06:24,292 --> 00:06:30,953
Bob Page, chair of the photo working group, to Wayne Hale, shuttle programme manager for launch integration.

83
00:06:30,953 --> 00:06:31,737
In person.

84
00:06:32,866 --> 00:06:35,025
Request two. Flight day six.

85
00:06:35,025 --> 00:06:41,068
Bob White, a contractor manager, to Lambert Austin, head of systems integration. By phone.

86
00:06:42,200 --> 00:06:44,231
Request three. Flight day six.

87
00:06:44,231 --> 00:06:49,514
Rodney Rocha, to Paul Shack, manager of the shuttle engineering office. By email.

88
00:06:50,633 --> 00:06:53,794
Rocha's email is worth reading, because of one word in it.

89
00:06:54,933 --> 00:07:00,079
Can we petition, and then in brackets, beg, for outside agency assistance.

90
00:07:01,200 --> 00:07:04,982
He was the chief engineer for the thermal protection system. The heat shield.

91
00:07:04,982 --> 00:07:09,638
The thing that keeps the vehicle from burning up. And he is using the word beg.

92
00:07:10,766 --> 00:07:15,538
None of the three requests went through the normal channel, which ran through mission management.

93
00:07:15,538 --> 00:07:19,674
The engineers went up the engineering side instead, because management was not engaging.

94
00:07:20,800 --> 00:07:24,196
And the board found that this routing was itself part of the failure.

95
00:07:25,333 --> 00:07:34,972
Routing the request through the engineering department led in part to it being viewed by shuttle programme managers as a non critical engineering desire, rather than a critical operational need.

96
00:07:36,100 --> 00:07:42,411
On the morning of the twenty second of January, the United States military began preparing to photograph Columbia in orbit.

97
00:07:42,411 --> 00:07:43,989
Ninety minutes later it stopped.

98
00:07:45,133 --> 00:07:50,540
Wayne Hale phoned a defence department representative at the Cape and asked the military to start the process.

99
00:07:51,666 --> 00:07:57,137
Within an hour, that request reached United States Strategic Command at Cheyenne Mountain, in Colorado.

100
00:07:57,137 --> 00:08:01,148
A plans officer there began identifying which assets could do it.

101
00:08:02,266 --> 00:08:04,225
This is real. It was happening.

102
00:08:04,225 --> 00:08:08,797
Somewhere in Colorado, people were working out how to point something at a wing.

103
00:08:09,933 --> 00:08:15,575
At half past eight that morning, NASA's liaison officer called Strategic Command back and cancelled it.

104
00:08:16,700 --> 00:08:22,421
The reason given was that NASA had identified its own in house resources and no longer needed the military's help.

105
00:08:23,566 --> 00:08:27,332
The board wrote the next sentence in a way that leaves nothing to interpret.

106
00:08:27,332 --> 00:08:33,519
The NASA request to the department of defense to prepare to image Columbia on orbit was both made and rescinded within ninety minutes.

107
00:08:34,666 --> 00:08:40,239
What happened inside those ninety minutes, the board describes carefully, and so will we.

108
00:08:40,239 --> 00:08:43,025
It says the following sequence likely occurred.

109
00:08:44,166 --> 00:08:49,014
The chair of the mission management team, Linda Ham, asked who was requesting the imagery.

110
00:08:49,014 --> 00:08:50,630
She called several senior managers.

111
00:08:50,630 --> 00:08:55,477
Each said they had not requested it, and could not identify a requirement for it.

112
00:08:56,600 --> 00:08:59,827
And that word, requirement, is where this comes apart.

113
00:08:59,827 --> 00:09:04,489
Because the engineers had not filed a requirement. They had asked a question.

114
00:09:05,633 --> 00:09:08,400
The board's finding is narrow and it is precise.

115
00:09:08,400 --> 00:09:13,012
Ham has publicly said she did not know the debris assessment team wanted the images.

116
00:09:13,012 --> 00:09:16,395
The board does not dispute that. What it says is this.

117
00:09:17,533 --> 00:09:22,679
She never asked them directly if the request was theirs, even though they were the team analysing the foam strike.

118
00:09:23,800 --> 00:09:27,849
There is one more finding here, and it is the one almost nobody quotes.

119
00:09:28,966 --> 00:09:35,106
Nobody in the chain of command had the security clearance to know what American imaging satellites could actually do.

120
00:09:35,106 --> 00:09:37,369
Not at NASA, not at the contractors.

121
00:09:37,369 --> 00:09:40,277
Nobody asked what quality of picture was even possible.

122
00:09:41,399 --> 00:09:46,504
So the decision not to look was made by people who did not know what looking would have shown them.

123
00:09:46,504 --> 00:09:51,353
The board's phrase is that they were making critical decisions about imagery capabilities based on little or no knowledge.

124
00:09:52,500 --> 00:09:57,259
On the twenty third of January, a flight director wrote an email closing the matter.

125
00:09:57,259 --> 00:10:01,701
The shuttle programme was asked directly if they wanted outside resources. They said no.

126
00:10:01,701 --> 00:10:05,509
After talking to Phil, I consider it to be a dead issue.

127
00:10:06,633 --> 00:10:10,065
The flight director who wrote that was LeRoy Cain. Remember the name.

128
00:10:10,065 --> 00:10:12,354
He comes back at the end of this.

129
00:10:13,500 --> 00:10:17,788
While all of that was happening, the engineers were doing the only thing left to them.

130
00:10:17,788 --> 00:10:20,735
They were trying to calculate the answer instead of photographing it.

131
00:10:21,866 --> 00:10:23,350
The tool was called Crater.

132
00:10:23,350 --> 00:10:28,397
It was a mathematical model for predicting how deep a piece of debris would penetrate the tiles.

133
00:10:29,533 --> 00:10:33,843
It had been built and validated for small debris. Ice, mostly.

134
00:10:33,843 --> 00:10:35,802
Pieces about three cubic inches.

135
00:10:36,933 --> 00:10:44,404
The piece that hit Columbia was, by the board's later estimate, about four hundred times larger than anything Crater had ever been tested against.

136
00:10:45,533 --> 00:10:49,086
The engineers running it that week thought the figure was six hundred and forty.

137
00:10:50,233 --> 00:10:54,159
Crater came back and said the debris would go deeper than the tile itself.

138
00:10:54,159 --> 00:10:55,562
Which would mean burn through.

139
00:10:56,700 --> 00:11:00,421
And here is the part that gets told wrong almost everywhere.

140
00:11:00,421 --> 00:11:03,466
Management did not overrule that result. The engineers did.

141
00:11:04,600 --> 00:11:06,006
They knew Crater ran conservative.

142
00:11:06,006 --> 00:11:09,661
They knew it did not model the hardened lower layer of the tile.

143
00:11:09,661 --> 00:11:14,161
So they talked themselves down, on reasoning that was defensible and turned out to be wrong.

144
00:11:15,300 --> 00:11:19,427
What they could not do was check that reasoning. To check it, they needed a picture.

145
00:11:20,566 --> 00:11:26,757
The board wrote one sentence about this situation that is, I think, the single most important line in the entire report.

146
00:11:27,900 --> 00:11:36,651
The debris assessment team was put in the untenable position of having to prove that a safety of flight issue existed, without the very images that would permit such a determination.

147
00:11:37,800 --> 00:11:41,603
Operations must be proved safe, rather than the other way around.

148
00:11:41,603 --> 00:11:43,677
NASA inverted this burden of proof.

149
00:11:44,800 --> 00:11:50,991
Meanwhile, two engineers were emailing each other about what would happen if the landing gear bay burned through on the way down.

150
00:11:52,133 --> 00:11:57,457
On the twenty seventh of January, a landing gear specialist at Langley wrote back to a colleague at Houston.

151
00:11:57,457 --> 00:12:01,380
I would bail out before I would let a loved one land like that.

152
00:12:02,500 --> 00:12:04,067
The next day he wrote again.

153
00:12:04,067 --> 00:12:09,814
Any more activity today on the tile damage, or are people just relegated to crossing their fingers and hoping for the best?

154
00:12:10,933 --> 00:12:15,060
Those emails went sideways, between two engineers. They did not go up.

155
00:12:16,200 --> 00:12:20,704
Programme rules said the mission management team would meet every day of a flight.

156
00:12:20,704 --> 00:12:23,279
During Columbia's sixteen days, it met five times.

157
00:12:24,400 --> 00:12:26,927
Two safety officials were contacted about the strike.

158
00:12:26,927 --> 00:12:30,402
One of them was the highest ranking safety official at NASA.

159
00:12:30,402 --> 00:12:34,509
The board's finding is that safety personnel took no actions to obtain imagery.

160
00:12:35,633 --> 00:12:39,604
And the crew. People often say the crew never knew. That is not true.

161
00:12:40,733 --> 00:12:51,977
On the twenty third of January a flight director emailed the commander and the pilot, sent them the video of the strike, and told them it was not even worth mentioning, and that there was absolutely no concern for entry.

162
00:12:51,977 --> 00:12:53,664
Husband wrote back and thanked him.

163
00:12:54,800 --> 00:12:56,058
They knew about the foam.

164
00:12:56,058 --> 00:13:02,349
What they were never told was that three people had asked for a photograph of their wing, and that the request had been called off.

165
00:13:03,466 --> 00:13:06,918
Saturday, the first of February, two thousand and three.

166
00:13:06,918 --> 00:13:11,904
Entry interface over the Pacific, eight forty four in the morning, eastern time.

167
00:13:13,033 --> 00:13:16,975
Columbia comes in over California, then Nevada, then New Mexico, trailing plasma.

168
00:13:16,975 --> 00:13:23,874
On the ground in Texas, people are filming it because it is a nice thing to watch on a Saturday morning.

169
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:30,329
At eight fifty four and twenty four seconds, a console officer in mission control calls the flight director.

170
00:13:31,466 --> 00:13:37,806
FYI, I've just lost four separate temperature transducers on the left side of the vehicle, hydraulic return temperatures.

171
00:13:38,933 --> 00:13:42,397
I mean, you're telling me you lost them all at exactly the same time?

172
00:13:43,533 --> 00:13:49,611
All four of them are located in the aft part of the left wing, right in front of the elevons, elevon actuators.

173
00:13:49,611 --> 00:13:50,992
And there is no commonality.

174
00:13:52,133 --> 00:13:52,808
No commonality.

175
00:13:52,808 --> 00:13:57,871
Meaning the sensors are not wired together, so a single wiring fault cannot explain it.

176
00:13:57,871 --> 00:14:00,571
Meaning something is happening to the left wing.

177
00:14:01,700 --> 00:14:05,897
We just lost tire pressure on the left outboard and left inboard, both tires.

178
00:14:07,033 --> 00:14:12,509
And Columbia, Houston, we see your tire pressure messages and we did not copy your last call.

179
00:14:13,633 --> 00:14:17,917
The answer comes back from the flight deck. One word, and the beginning of another.

180
00:14:19,066 --> 00:14:24,944
The report prints it as: Roger. And then, in square brackets, cut off in mid word.

181
00:14:26,066 --> 00:14:29,621
Eight fifty nine and thirty two seconds. Loss of signal.

182
00:14:29,621 --> 00:14:33,175
Which, at that point in an entry, is completely normal.

183
00:14:33,175 --> 00:14:36,019
It happens every flight when the antennas switch.

184
00:14:37,166 --> 00:14:41,032
So nobody in that room reacts. They wait, the way they always wait.

185
00:14:42,166 --> 00:14:45,997
Forty six seconds later, the vehicle comes apart over east Texas.

186
00:14:45,997 --> 00:14:50,525
Mission control has no idea. They are still working the tyre pressure problem.

187
00:14:51,666 --> 00:15:02,664
For twelve more minutes, the loop carries an ordinary technical conversation about instrumentation, while outside, in Nacogdoches and Hemphill and Lufkin, people are hearing what sounds like thunder on a clear day.

188
00:15:03,800 --> 00:15:11,489
At about twelve minutes past nine, a member of the flight control team gets a call on his mobile phone from someone who has just seen it on television.

189
00:15:11,489 --> 00:15:14,406
He walks over to the flight director's console and tells him.

190
00:15:15,533 --> 00:15:19,974
Flight Director LeRoy Cain calls for the ground control officer. He has to call twice.

191
00:15:21,100 --> 00:15:22,245
Lock the doors.

192
00:15:23,366 --> 00:15:27,820
Then he tells the room: no phone calls off site. No data in or out.

193
00:15:27,820 --> 00:15:32,274
Every console begins archiving its own data, because from this moment the room is evidence.

194
00:15:33,400 --> 00:15:38,533
What follows is the part the accident board actually spent most of its time on.

195
00:15:38,533 --> 00:15:40,244
Not the foam. The organisation.

196
00:15:41,366 --> 00:15:42,349
First, the search.

197
00:15:42,349 --> 00:15:46,281
More than twenty five thousand people from two hundred and seventy organisations.

198
00:15:46,281 --> 00:15:52,834
Over one and a half million hours, across two point three million acres, an area approaching the size of Connecticut.

199
00:15:53,966 --> 00:15:58,190
Eighty four thousand pieces were recovered. Eighty four thousand, nine hundred pounds.

200
00:15:58,190 --> 00:16:00,653
Thirty eight per cent of the orbiter.

201
00:16:01,800 --> 00:16:06,757
Satellites, infrared, hyperspectral imaging, ground penetrating radar and U two aircraft were all used.

202
00:16:06,757 --> 00:16:10,298
The board's assessment is that they proved of little value.

203
00:16:10,298 --> 00:16:14,548
It came down to people walking in a line through the woods.

204
00:16:15,666 --> 00:16:22,932
On the twenty seventh of March, a helicopter searching Angelina National Forest suffered a mechanical failure and went down at treetop height.

205
00:16:22,932 --> 00:16:24,913
It was too low to autorotate.

206
00:16:26,033 --> 00:16:32,720
Jules Mier, a contract pilot, and Charles Krenek, a Texas Forest Service aviation specialist, were killed.

207
00:16:32,720 --> 00:16:34,392
Three others were injured.

208
00:16:35,533 --> 00:16:42,203
The accident board put both of their names on the dedication page of its report, with their job titles, underneath the seven astronauts.

209
00:16:42,203 --> 00:16:46,844
Nine people died because of this accident, and the report says so on the first page.

210
00:16:47,966 --> 00:16:52,250
Then, in July, they went to San Antonio and fired the foam at the wing.

211
00:16:53,400 --> 00:17:01,759
One point six seven pounds of foam, at seven hundred and seventy seven feet per second, into a panel taken from Atlantis that had flown twenty six missions.

212
00:17:02,900 --> 00:17:07,498
A hole roughly sixteen inches by seventeen. Cracks running eleven inches out from it.

213
00:17:08,633 --> 00:17:14,197
And the board wrote down what the test was actually for, which is the most revealing sentence in that whole chapter.

214
00:17:15,333 --> 00:17:23,692
The impact tests established that foam can breach the reinforced carbon carbon, and also counteracted the lingering denial or discounting of the analytic evidence.

215
00:17:24,833 --> 00:17:28,935
They did not fire that gun to find out whether foam could break a wing.

216
00:17:28,935 --> 00:17:30,576
The analysis already said it could.

217
00:17:30,576 --> 00:17:33,584
They fired it to end an argument inside their own organisation.

218
00:17:34,733 --> 00:17:46,619
Because five months earlier, four days after the accident, the shuttle programme manager had told a press conference that it doesn't make sense to us that a piece of debris could be the root cause of the loss of Columbia and its crew.

219
00:17:47,766 --> 00:17:49,024
That was not a lie.

220
00:17:49,024 --> 00:17:55,315
It was what the analysis appeared to show, to people who had watched foam fall off that tank for twenty two years without anyone dying.

221
00:17:56,433 --> 00:18:00,404
The board's conclusion is one sentence and it is the reason this episode exists.

222
00:18:01,533 --> 00:18:08,064
In the board's view, NASA's organisational culture and structure had as much to do with this accident as the external tank foam.

223
00:18:09,200 --> 00:18:12,831
And they knew how that would be received, so they wrote a warning about it first.

224
00:18:13,966 --> 00:18:17,130
Many accident investigations make the same mistake in defining causes.

225
00:18:17,130 --> 00:18:22,508
They identify the widget that broke, then locate the person most closely connected with the technical failure.

226
00:18:22,508 --> 00:18:25,355
The board did not want to make these errors.

227
00:18:26,500 --> 00:18:27,492
Nobody was prosecuted.

228
00:18:27,492 --> 00:18:37,419
Some managers were reassigned that July, and NASA said publicly on the day that the changes were no reflection on the competence or diligence or commitment or professionalism of anybody.

229
00:18:38,566 --> 00:18:41,795
So the organisation restructured while stating that no individual was at fault.

230
00:18:41,795 --> 00:18:43,947
Which is exactly what the board asked for.

231
00:18:43,947 --> 00:18:49,328
And it is also why no one was ever held to account. Both of those things are true at once.

232
00:18:50,466 --> 00:18:54,437
There is one more thing in this report, and it is the strangest fact I found.

233
00:18:55,566 --> 00:19:00,925
In nineteen ninety six, a sociologist named Diane Vaughan published a book about the Challenger accident.

234
00:19:00,925 --> 00:19:08,294
She argued that the disaster was not caused by villains, but by an organisation slowly agreeing that a dangerous thing was normal.

235
00:19:08,294 --> 00:19:10,639
She called it the normalisation of deviance.

236
00:19:11,766 --> 00:19:16,912
In April two thousand and three, she was called to testify before the Columbia board, in Houston.

237
00:19:18,033 --> 00:19:19,992
Then they hired her onto the staff.

238
00:19:21,133 --> 00:19:24,712
And then she personally wrote chapter eight of the report.

239
00:19:24,712 --> 00:19:28,291
The chapter is called History as Cause: Columbia and Challenger.

240
00:19:29,433 --> 00:19:33,795
The woman who diagnosed the first accident was brought in to write the finding that nobody had listened.

241
00:19:34,933 --> 00:19:36,340
Her chapter says this.

242
00:19:36,340 --> 00:19:40,915
The causes of the institutional failure responsible for Challenger have not been fixed.

243
00:19:40,915 --> 00:19:46,897
And second, that if these systemic flaws are not resolved, the scene is set for another accident.

244
00:19:48,033 --> 00:19:53,476
There is an image in that chapter I have not been able to stop thinking about.

245
00:19:53,476 --> 00:20:02,322
Engineers and managers incorporated worsening anomalies into the engineering experience base, which functioned as an elastic waistband, expanding to hold larger deviations from the original design.

246
00:20:03,466 --> 00:20:06,946
The word echoes, which that chapter turns on, came from Sally Ride.

247
00:20:06,946 --> 00:20:12,747
She sat on the Challenger commission in nineteen eighty six and on the Columbia board in two thousand and three.

248
00:20:12,747 --> 00:20:14,777
The only person who ever did both.

249
00:20:15,900 --> 00:20:20,170
And the closest parallel the report draws between the two accidents is a single sentence.

250
00:20:20,170 --> 00:20:27,289
Challenger astronauts were told that the cold temperature was not a problem, and Columbia astronauts were told that the foam strike was not a problem.

251
00:20:28,433 --> 00:20:30,397
The board made twenty nine recommendations.

252
00:20:30,397 --> 00:20:33,344
Fifteen had to be done before anyone flew again.

253
00:20:34,466 --> 00:20:38,672
An independent task group later found NASA met the intent of twelve of them.

254
00:20:38,672 --> 00:20:39,873
Three, it could not.

255
00:20:41,000 --> 00:20:43,492
One of the three was hardening the orbiter's wing.

256
00:20:43,492 --> 00:20:52,076
And the stated reason it was dropped is this: the long term plan was abandoned after the national policy decision to retire the space shuttle fleet no later than twenty ten.

257
00:20:53,200 --> 00:20:57,614
Which is worth sitting with. The accident board never recommended retiring the shuttle.

258
00:20:57,614 --> 00:21:02,029
It said: if you fly past twenty ten, recertify it first. A conditional.

259
00:21:03,166 --> 00:21:07,521
One hundred and fifty four days later the White House turned that conditional into a deadline.

260
00:21:07,521 --> 00:21:11,604
And the retirement decision then cancelled one of the fixes the accident had called for.

261
00:21:12,733 --> 00:21:16,847
In July two thousand and five, Discovery flew the return to flight mission.

262
00:21:16,847 --> 00:21:19,062
The tank shed foam again. It missed.

263
00:21:19,062 --> 00:21:23,809
NASA grounded the fleet the following day, and did not fly again for eleven months.

264
00:21:24,933 --> 00:21:27,476
Columbia itself is still at the Kennedy Space Center.

265
00:21:27,476 --> 00:21:32,561
Eighty four thousand pieces of it, in a room on the sixteenth floor of the Vehicle Assembly Building.

266
00:21:33,700 --> 00:21:35,220
And they lend it out.

267
00:21:35,220 --> 00:21:39,478
Researchers studying how spacecraft come apart during re entry can borrow pieces of it.

268
00:21:39,478 --> 00:21:42,216
Three people have written doctorates based on that debris.

269
00:21:43,333 --> 00:21:47,017
Challenger's recovered structure was sealed into a disused missile silo.

270
00:21:47,017 --> 00:21:49,596
Columbia's is signed out, studied, and returned.

271
00:21:49,596 --> 00:21:54,017
It is the only destroyed spacecraft in history that is still working.

272
00:21:55,166 --> 00:22:00,020
One last thing. Ilan Ramon carried a copy of a pencil drawing into orbit with him.

273
00:22:00,020 --> 00:22:04,570
It is called Moon Landscape, and it shows the Earth as seen from the moon.

274
00:22:05,700 --> 00:22:13,045
It was drawn in nineteen forty two by a fourteen year old boy named Petr Ginz, in the Theresienstadt ghetto, from imagination.

275
00:22:13,045 --> 00:22:16,384
He was murdered at Auschwitz two years later, aged sixteen.

276
00:22:17,533 --> 00:22:20,472
Yad Vashem made Ramon a facsimile to take with him.

277
00:22:20,472 --> 00:22:23,411
The original never left Jerusalem, and it is still there.

278
00:22:24,533 --> 00:22:28,089
Petr Ginz was born on the first of February, nineteen twenty eight.

279
00:22:28,089 --> 00:22:34,015
Columbia came apart on the first of February, two thousand and three, which would have been his seventy fifth birthday.

280
00:22:35,133 --> 00:22:39,388
Three people asked for a photograph of that wing. The photograph was never taken.

281
00:22:39,388 --> 00:22:41,820
Everything else in this story follows from that.

282
00:22:42,966 --> 00:22:49,924
If the way an organisation talks itself past a warning is the part that stays with you, we made the other half of this story.

283
00:22:49,924 --> 00:22:54,656
Watch Engineers Said Don't Launch, Seventy Three Seconds Later Challenger Was Gone, right here on this channel.

284
00:22:54,656 --> 00:22:58,274
And subscribe to Black Box Breakdown, so you don't miss the next one.

